From 19d7e97b652e70d83b0738ffce631203dc8153fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:48:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Documentation: fixes found by the check of the review

hip-clang on the AMD platform still requires HIP and clang++; a reference derived from a variable given by TFEL_STATIC_VARIABLE (and not one bound directly to it) is only valid until the end of the full expression; the restriction of the device code applies to any constexpr static variable defined in host code, not only to the ones of a class type.
---
 docs/web/generic-parallel-material-property-interface.md | 4 ++--
 docs/web/release-notes/release-notes-5.2.md              | 5 +++--
 docs/web/tfel-config.md                                  | 6 +++---
 3 files changed, 8 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/web/generic-parallel-material-property-interface.md b/docs/web/generic-parallel-material-property-interface.md
index c465289ac..f2f61ed4c 100644
--- a/docs/web/generic-parallel-material-property-interface.md
+++ b/docs/web/generic-parallel-material-property-interface.md
@@ -728,8 +728,8 @@ The following tests configurations are currently supported:
 - `hip-hipcc`: compilation of `HIP` files using the `hipcc` compiler.
 - `hip-clang`: compilation of `HIP` files using the `clang++` compiler,
   only on the NVIDIA platform (`HIP_PLATFORM=nvidia`). On the AMD
-  platform, this configuration is accepted but ignored: no configuration
-  file is installed and no test is declared.
+  platform, this configuration installs no configuration file and
+  declares no test, but `HIP` and `clang++` are still required.
 - `stlpar-parunseq-gcc`, `stlpar-parunseq-clang`, `stlpar-parunseq-icpx`:
   parallel algorithms of the standard library with the `par_unseq`
   execution policy on the threads of the host (`g++`, `clang++` and `icpx`
diff --git a/docs/web/release-notes/release-notes-5.2.md b/docs/web/release-notes/release-notes-5.2.md
index 55d6680df..3793494d2 100644
--- a/docs/web/release-notes/release-notes-5.2.md
+++ b/docs/web/release-notes/release-notes-5.2.md
@@ -842,8 +842,9 @@ are shared with the `generic-parallel` interface for material properties.
   `TFEL_STATIC_VARIABLE(<class>::<name>)`, which gives a temporary copy
   of the variable in device code (and in the whole translation unit with
   the compilers translating host and device code in a single pass), since
-  device code can't refer to a constexpr static variable of a class type,
-  such as a quantity. The address of such a variable can't be taken, and
+  device code can't refer to a constexpr static variable defined in host
+  code (for instance a quantity given to an operator taking its arguments
+  by reference). The address of such a variable can't be taken, and
   a reference derived from it is only valid until the end of the full
   expression (see the [`TFEL/Config` page](tfel-config.html)).
 - the `device_compatible_class` DSL option requires a behaviour class
diff --git a/docs/web/tfel-config.md b/docs/web/tfel-config.md
index 0b40579f4..2e588a084 100644
--- a/docs/web/tfel-config.md
+++ b/docs/web/tfel-config.md
@@ -40,9 +40,9 @@ The `TFEL/Config/TFELConfig.hxx` header defines the following macros:
   the host, a temporary copy `(decltype(v){v})` when `TFEL_DEVICE_CODE` is
   defined, since device code can't refer to a constexpr static variable
   defined in host code. `MFront` uses it for the static variables read by
-  the code blocks of the behaviours: a reference bound to such a variable
-  is only valid until the end of the full expression, and its address
-  can't be taken.
+  the code blocks of the behaviours: a reference derived from such a
+  variable, for instance the result of `std::min(Tref, T)`, is only valid
+  until the end of the full expression, and its address can't be taken.
 
 `TFEL_DEVICE_CODE` allows to select the code which can't be executed on
 the device, for instance when throwing an exception, while keeping the
